{% doc %}
  Policy sidebar — the summary column beside /policies/*, and the figures,
  journeys, clauses and hand-offs each of the five documents carries.

  ── Why this is not a template ────────────────────────────────────────
  It should have been. It cannot be. `policy` is not one of Shopify's
  template types: there is no policy.json (the CLI rejects it outright —
  "Template type 'policy' does not support JSON templates") and there is
  no working policy.liquid either. A policy.liquid file uploads without
  complaint and is then simply never used — the storefront goes on
  rendering Shopify's own fixed markup:

      <div class="shopify-policy__container">
        <div class="shopify-policy__title"><h1>…</h1></div>
        <div class="shopify-policy__body"><div class="rte">…</div></div>
      </div>

  straight into {{ content_for_layout }}. Dawn ships no policy template
  for the same reason. So the theme cannot own this page's markup; it can
  only dress that container (sv-system.css § POLICY PAGES) and add to it
  from the outside, which is what this section is.

  It is rendered from layout/theme.liquid, immediately before
  {{ content_for_layout }} and only on /policies/*, and CSS then lays the
  two out as one two-column page. Nothing here can be placed *inside*
  Shopify's container, which is why the summary is a column beside the
  document rather than a band under its title.

  ── One section, five documents ───────────────────────────────────────
  There is one policy route and five policies, and no template to hang
  per-policy settings on, so the branch is the request path. `handle`
  below is that branch, and `scope` is what every conditional here reads.

  Three things are built from the document itself and so are shared by
  all five: the eyebrow, the last-updated stamp, and the jump list. The
  figures, the journey, the clause and the hand-off are per policy, and
  every setting that carries them is named <prefix><role> — sh_, rf_,
  pv_, tm_, ct_ — so that a sixth policy is a prefix and a schema header
  and no new markup. Blocks carry a `policy` setting for the same reason:
  one section cannot have two block lists.

  ── What each document gets, and why it differs ───────────────────────
  Each page is furnished from what a person actually arrives at it to
  find, which is different on each one:

  · Shipping is a page of figures — when it leaves, what it costs, when
    it lands — so it gets three figures and the parcel's journey.
  · Refund is a page of conditions, and two of them decide every claim
    before the rest matters: you have 24 hours, and you must have filmed
    the unboxing. Both are sentences inside §4, read fourth, by someone
    who has already opened the packet.
  · Privacy is read by a parent deciding whether to hand over a name and
    an address. The three figures are the three answers to that (the card
    details we never see, the data we never sell, the 30 days), and §2's
    paragraph on what we deliberately do not ask about a child — the most
    unusual commitment in the document — becomes the dark card. §7's
    rights become a journey, because a right you cannot see the steps of
    is not one anybody exercises.
  · Terms is read for what it commits you to: prepaid, 18+, liability
    capped at the order. §5's "the contract forms when we confirm
    dispatch" is the line nobody reads and the one that matters, so it
    becomes step 03 of a four-step journey. The dark card is §3's
    AI-imagery disclosure — material to what you think you are buying,
    and currently sitting behind two paragraphs about screen colour.
  · Contact is not a legal document at all and is the one page on the
    site a reader can act on wrongly: the address is an office, not a
    shop. That becomes the dark card, and the hand-off becomes what the
    page was missing entirely — a WhatsApp button and a mailto, rather
    than a phone number and an address set as body copy.

  What is NOT restated here, on any of them, is any list the document
  argues a case over — Refund §2 and §3, the shipping rate table,
  Privacy's retention schedule. A second copy in a sidebar is how a store
  ends up refusing on one page what it accepts on another.

  The policy text itself is never touched. It comes from Settings →
  Policies and it is the authoritative version. The three things the
  script does to it — hiding the restated title, lifting the stamp,
  giving the headings ids — each move or reveal what Admin wrote. None
  of them changes a word of it.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  # The only thing that tells the five policies apart: there is no
  # template, so there is no `template` and no page object to ask.
  # request.path is '/policies/shipping-policy' and, under a localised
  # domain, '/en-in/policies/shipping-policy' — `contains` covers both.
  assign path = request.path | downcase
  assign handle = 'policy'

  if path contains 'shipping-policy'
    assign handle = 'shipping-policy'
  elsif path contains 'refund-policy'
    assign handle = 'refund-policy'
  elsif path contains 'privacy-policy'
    assign handle = 'privacy-policy'
  elsif path contains 'terms-of-service'
    assign handle = 'terms-of-service'
  elsif path contains 'terms-of-sale'
    assign handle = 'terms-of-sale'
  elsif path contains 'contact-information'
    assign handle = 'contact-information'
  elsif path contains 'legal-notice'
    assign handle = 'legal-notice'
  elsif path contains 'subscription-policy'
    assign handle = 'subscription-policy'
  endif

  # Blank on any policy this theme has not furnished — terms of sale,
  # legal notice, subscription policy — which then get the eyebrow, the
  # stamp and the contents list and nothing else. Every conditional below
  # reads this rather than `handle`, so a sixth furnished policy is one
  # more branch here and no new markup.
  assign scope = ''
  if handle == 'shipping-policy'
    assign scope = 'shipping'
  elsif handle == 'refund-policy'
    assign scope = 'refund'
  elsif handle == 'privacy-policy'
    assign scope = 'privacy'
  elsif handle == 'terms-of-service'
    assign scope = 'terms'
  elsif handle == 'contact-information'
    assign scope = 'contact'
  endif

  # The eyebrow says what the document is FOR, where Shopify's own <h1>
  # says what it is called. Hard-coded rather than a setting, because a
  # setting here would have to read the same on all five pages.
  case handle
    when 'shipping-policy'
      assign eyebrow = 'Shipping & delivery'
    when 'refund-policy'
      assign eyebrow = 'Returns & claims'
    when 'privacy-policy'
      assign eyebrow = 'Your privacy'
    when 'terms-of-service'
      assign eyebrow = 'Terms'
    when 'terms-of-sale'
      assign eyebrow = 'Terms'
    when 'contact-information'
      assign eyebrow = 'Reaching us'
    else
      assign eyebrow = 'Our policies'
  endcase

  # ── One set of furniture, five sets of words for it ─────────────────
  # Five furnished policies × eleven strings is fifty-five settings, and
  # written as `if scope == …` branches that is fifty-five assigns in a
  # ladder nobody will keep in step. Every setting is instead named
  # <prefix><role> — sh_help_title, pv_rule_body — and read through one
  # lookup, so a sixth policy is a prefix here, a header in the schema,
  # and not one line of this file.
  #
  # Bracket access takes a variable, not an expression, which is why each
  # key is captured first. Verbose, but it is the only form that works.
  case scope
    when 'shipping'
      assign px = 'sh_'
    when 'refund'
      assign px = 'rf_'
    when 'privacy'
      assign px = 'pv_'
    when 'terms'
      assign px = 'tm_'
    when 'contact'
      assign px = 'ct_'
    else
      assign px = ''
  endcase

  if px != blank
    assign k = px | append: 'flow_title'
    assign flow_title = section.settings[k]

    assign k = px | append: 'rule_label'
    assign rule_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'rule_title'
    assign rule_title = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'rule_body'
    assign rule_body = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'rule_link_label'
    assign rule_link_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'rule_link_url'
    assign rule_link_url = section.settings[k]

    assign k = px | append: 'help_label'
    assign help_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_title'
    assign help_title = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_body'
    assign help_body = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_wa_label'
    assign help_wa_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_wa_text'
    assign help_wa_text = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_mail_label'
    assign help_mail_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_link_label'
    assign help_link_label = section.settings[k]
    assign k = px | append: 'help_link_url'
    assign help_link_url = section.settings[k]
  endif

  # Both read from Admin, never typed — the rule sv-credentials states and
  # the announcement bar keeps. A policy that prints the shop's own
  # contact details from a theme setting is how two pages come to give two
  # different numbers.
  assign whatsapp = shop.metafields.custom.whatsapp_number.value
  assign support_email = shop.metafields.custom.support_email.value
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Arms the entrance before anything paints — see the snippet's own note on
  why this must be the first child. Its parent here is the section's own
  wrapper div (.sv-pol-mount), which is therefore what carries
  .sv-motion-ready, and what the entrance rules key off.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'sv-motion-gate' -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Two top-level blocks, not one, and the wrapper between them is set to
  display:contents so both become grid items of <main> alongside Shopify's
  container. That is what lets the document sit BETWEEN them: beside the
  figures on desktop, and after them on a phone, where a single column
  would otherwise have pushed the page's own title three screens down.
{%- endcomment -%}
<aside class="sv-pol sv-pol--{{ handle }}" data-sv aria-label="{{ eyebrow | escape }} — at a glance">

  <p class="eyebrow sv-pol__eyebrow" data-sv-motion>{{ eyebrow }}</p>

  {%- comment -%}
    ── The stamp ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Empty in markup and filled by script from the document's own first
    line. "Last updated: 31 August 2026" is currently body copy, in the
    same face and size as the eleven sections under it — and it is the
    one fact a person checks before deciding whether the rest is worth
    reading. It belongs at the top of the column, not fourth in the
    reading order. Hidden until filled, and on a document that carries
    no such line (Contact) it stays hidden.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <p class="sv-pol__stamp" data-sv-stamp hidden></p>

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Contents ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Empty in markup and filled by script from the document's own <h3>s.
    A policy's sections live in an Admin RTE this theme never sees, so a
    hand-written list would fall out of step the first time a section was
    added. Hidden until filled: a contents list with nothing in it is
    worse than none.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <nav class="sv-pol__toc" aria-label="Sections of this policy" data-sv-toc hidden>
    <p class="lab lab--grey sv-pol__toclab">In this policy</p>
    <ul class="sv-pol__toclist" data-sv-toclist></ul>
  </nav>

  {%- if scope != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      ── Pins ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The three figures people open this page to find, beside the eight
      or ten numbered sections they would otherwise have to read to find
      them. On Shipping: dispatch, delivery, free-shipping threshold —
      deliberately NOT the rate table, which stays in the policy body.
      On Refund: the 24-hour clock, when the money lands, and who pays
      the return postage. Those three are what a claim turns on, and
      all three are sentences buried in §4 and §5 of the document.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign pin_count = 0 -%}
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- if block.type == 'pin' -%}
        {%- assign bscope = block.settings.policy | default: 'shipping' -%}
        {%- if bscope == scope -%}{%- assign pin_count = pin_count | plus: 1 -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- if pin_count > 0 -%}
      <ul class="sv-pol__pins" data-sv-motion>
        {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
          {%- assign bscope = block.settings.policy | default: 'shipping' -%}
          {%- if block.type == 'pin' and bscope == scope -%}
            {%- liquid
              # A figure that also lives in a metafield is read from the
              # metafield, never typed — the rule the announcement bar
              # holds itself to, and for the same reason: a typed "₹499"
              # here is exactly how the site comes to disagree with
              # itself. The typed figure stays as the fallback.
              assign pin_value = block.settings.value

              if block.settings.source == 'threshold'
                assign threshold = shop.metafields.custom.free_shipping_threshold.value
                if threshold != blank
                  # A money metafield's .value is already in the subunit,
                  # so the filter takes it raw. Verified on the live shop.
                  assign pin_value = threshold | money_without_trailing_zeros
                endif
              elsif block.settings.source == 'kerala'
                assign kerala = shop.metafields.custom.delivery_charge_kerala.value
                if kerala != blank
                  # number_integer, i.e. rupees, so it is scaled up.
                  assign pin_value = kerala | times: 100 | round | money_without_trailing_zeros
                endif
              endif
            -%}
            <li class="sv-pol__pin" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
              <span class="lab">{{ block.settings.label }}</span>
              <strong class="sv-pol__pinval">{{ pin_value }}</strong>
              {%- if block.settings.note != blank -%}
                <span class="sv-pol__pinnote">{{ block.settings.note }}</span>
              {%- endif -%}
            </li>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if rule_title != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      ── The one clause, stated once, loudly ─────────────────────────
      One per policy, and never more: the page's single dark object only
      works while there is exactly one of it.

      It is for the clause that decides something before any of the rest
      of the document matters, and that the document itself buries. On
      Refund it is the unboxing video, read fourth by someone who has
      already opened the packet. On Privacy it is the paragraph in §2
      that says we ask for nothing about the child — the most unusual
      commitment on the page and the one a parent came to find. On Terms
      it is the AI-imagery disclosure in §3, which is material to what
      you think you are buying and sits behind two paragraphs about
      screen colour. On Contact it is that the address is not a shop,
      which is the only line on the page a reader can act on wrongly and
      end up somewhere in Thiruvananthapuram with nothing to collect.

      Shipping has none. Nothing on it qualifies, and an emerald card
      invented to fill the slot would teach the reader that the dark
      object means nothing in particular.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="sv-pol__rule" data-sv-motion>
      {%- if rule_label != blank -%}
        <p class="lab">{{ rule_label }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
      <p class="sv-pol__ruleh">{{ rule_title }}</p>
      {%- if rule_body != blank -%}
        <div class="sv-pol__rulebody">{{ rule_body }}</div>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if rule_link_url != blank and rule_link_label != blank -%}
        <a class="sv-pol__rulelink" href="{{ rule_link_url }}">
          {{ rule_link_label }}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</aside>

{%- if scope != blank -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ── After the document ────────────────────────────────────────────
    The second block. On desktop it continues the left column beneath
    the figures; on a phone it falls below the policy, which is the
    right place for "what happens next" and for the hand-off.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sv-pol__after" data-sv>

    {%- comment -%}
      ── The journey ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
      What actually happens, in order.

      On Shipping the policy says all of this across sections 2, 4 and 7;
      read as prose it is three separate facts, and read as a line it is
      one process ending in the unboxing film, which is where it needs to
      land.

      On Refund it is §5, which is already a list — but a list of five
      bullets reads as five conditions, and what it actually describes is
      four moves with a clock on each one. "Within 1 working day", "at
      our cost", "your choice", "within 7 working days" is the whole
      shape of the thing, and it is the shape that answers the question
      someone with a torn garment is really asking.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign step_count = 0 -%}
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- if block.type == 'step' -%}
        {%- assign bscope = block.settings.policy | default: 'shipping' -%}
        {%- if bscope == scope -%}{%- assign step_count = step_count | plus: 1 -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- if step_count > 0 and flow_title != blank -%}
      <section class="sv-pol__flow" aria-labelledby="sv-pol-flow-h" data-sv-motion>
        <h2 class="lab lab--grey sv-pol__flowh" id="sv-pol-flow-h">{{ flow_title }}</h2>
        <ol class="sv-pol__steps">
          {%- comment -%}
            The thread the steps hang from: four numerals on one line
            read as a sequence, four stacked read as a list.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="sv-pol__thread" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {%- comment -%}
            Counted separately, not from forloop.index: the loop walks
            every block in the section — both policies' pins and both
            policies' steps — so anything ahead of these would otherwise
            number the journey 09 to 12.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign step_n = 0 -%}
          {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
            {%- assign bscope = block.settings.policy | default: 'shipping' -%}
            {%- if block.type == 'step' and bscope == scope -%}
              {%- assign step_n = step_n | plus: 1 -%}
              <li class="sv-pol__step" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
                <span class="sv-pol__stepnum" aria-hidden="true">
                  {{- step_n | prepend: '0' | slice: -2, 2 -}}
                </span>
                <span class="sv-pol__steplab">{{ block.settings.label }}</span>
                {%- if block.settings.detail != blank -%}
                  <span class="sv-pol__stepdet">{{ block.settings.detail }}</span>
                {%- endif -%}
              </li>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        </ol>
      </section>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if help_title != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        ── Where to go next ──────────────────────────────────────────
        A policy answers "what are the rules"; it does not answer "my
        parcel has not moved in three days", and it does not fill in a
        claim for you. This is the hand-off to the places that do —
        on Refund, to /pages/report-a-problem, which is the whole of
        §4 turned into something you can work through with a phone in
        the other hand.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="sv-pol__help" data-sv-motion>
        {%- if help_label != blank -%}<p class="lab">{{ help_label }}</p>{%- endif -%}
        <p class="sv-pol__helph">{{ help_title }}</p>
        {%- if help_body != blank -%}
          <div class="sv-pol__helpbody">{{ help_body }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if whatsapp != blank and help_wa_label != blank -%}
          <a class="btn sv-pol__helpbtn" href="https://wa.me/{{ whatsapp }}?text={{ help_wa_text | url_encode }}" rel="noopener" target="_blank">
            {{ help_wa_label }}
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Email, where the document itself names email as the channel.
          §7 of the Privacy policy says "write to info@…" and the Data
          Protection Act expects a written request; a WhatsApp button
          alone would be the theme quietly contradicting the policy it
          is sitting beside. The address is never typed — it is the same
          metafield sv-credentials prints in the footer, so the two can
          never disagree, and with the field empty the button is simply
          not there.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if support_email != blank and help_mail_label != blank -%}
          {%- liquid
            # Solid where it is the card's only action (Privacy, where
            # §7 names writing as the channel and there is no WhatsApp
            # button beside it), ghost where WhatsApp already carries the
            # weight. Two filled buttons in a 270px column read as two
            # competing instructions.
            assign mail_class = 'btn sv-pol__helpbtn'
            if whatsapp != blank and help_wa_label != blank
              assign mail_class = 'btn btn--ghost sv-pol__helpbtn sv-pol__helpmail'
            endif
          -%}
          <a class="{{ mail_class }}" href="mailto:{{ support_email }}">
            {{ help_mail_label }}
          </a>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if help_link_url != blank and help_link_label != blank -%}
          <a class="act sv-pol__helplink" href="{{ help_link_url }}">{{ help_link_label }}</a>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

<script>
    (function () {
      /* The section's own wrapper div: both blocks above are its
         children, and it is what the motion gate has already put
         .sv-motion-ready on. */
      var root = document.currentScript.parentElement;
      if (!root) return;

      var calm = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
      root.classList.add('is-js');

      /* ── Sticky offset ──────────────────────────────────────────────
         Anchors have to land clear of the sticky header group, whose
         height is not knowable in CSS — it changes with the
         announcement bar and between breakpoints. The CSS value stays
         the fallback. Written to the documentElement rather than to
         this column, because what it offsets (the policy headings) is
         Shopify's markup, outside this element. */
      var chrome = document.getElementById('header-group');
      function setStick() {
        if (!chrome) return;
        var h = Math.round(chrome.getBoundingClientRect().height);
        if (h > 0) document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--sv-stick', h + 'px');
      }
      setStick();
      window.addEventListener('resize', setStick);

      /* ── Contents, read out of the document ─────────────────────────
         Every policy is a run of numbered <h3> sections inside one RTE
         blob, with no ids of its own. Reading the headings back out and
         slugging them is the only way a contents list can stay true to
         Admin copy the theme never sees.

         Deferred, and it has to be: this column is rendered BEFORE
         {{ content_for_layout }}, so at the moment this script runs the
         policy it is describing has not been parsed yet and every
         query for it comes back empty. */
      /* ── The title, printed twice ───────────────────────────────────
         Every one of the five policies opens its Admin body with a
         heading that restates the page title Shopify has already
         printed as the <h1> directly above it: "Terms of service" then
         "Terms of Service", "Privacy policy" then "Privacy Policy",
         "Contact information" then "Contact Us". Two titles, two sizes,
         forty pixels apart, on every policy page the store has.

         It cannot be fixed in Admin without the policies reading
         headless in Shopify's own emails and exports, where that
         heading is the only title there is. So it is removed here,
         where the <h1> is present, and only here.

         The test is the first significant word, matched against the
         <h1>'s. It is deliberately narrower than it could be: an exact
         match would leave "Contact Us" and "Refund, Return &
         Replacement Policy" standing, and anything looser would start
         eating real section headings the day a policy opens with one.
         No match, no change — the document is Admin's, not ours. */
      function dropRestatedTitle(body) {
        var h1 = document.querySelector('.shopify-policy__title h1');
        var rte = body.querySelector('.rte') || body;
        var first = rte.firstElementChild;

        if (!h1 || !first || !/^H[12]$/.test(first.tagName)) return;

        /* A heading that is not the document's first is a section of
           it, whatever it happens to be called. */
        if (rte.querySelector('h1, h2, h3') !== first) return;

        var word = function (el) {
          var m = (el.textContent || '').toLowerCase().match(/[a-z0-9]+/);
          return m ? m[0] : '';
        };

        if (word(first) && word(first) === word(h1)) first.hidden = true;
      }

      /* ── The stamp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
         "Last updated: 31 August 2026" is the document's second line and
         is set as body copy, which is to say it is invisible. Lifted to
         the top of the column, where the one question a returning reader
         has — is this the version I already read — is answered before
         the document starts.

         Copied, then the original hidden: the same fact twice on one
         screen reads as an editing mistake. Matched on its own wording
         only, so a policy that does not carry the line (Contact) is left
         exactly as Admin wrote it. */
      function liftStamp(body) {
        var slot = root.querySelector('[data-sv-stamp]');
        var rte = body.querySelector('.rte') || body;
        if (!slot) return;

        var kids = Array.prototype.slice.call(rte.children).slice(0, 4);

        for (var i = 0; i < kids.length; i++) {
          var el = kids[i];
          if (el.tagName !== 'P') continue;

          var text = (el.textContent || '').trim();
          if (!/^last updated\b/i.test(text)) continue;
          /* The line and nothing else — a paragraph that opens with it
             and then carries a sentence is prose, not a stamp. */
          if (text.length > 48) continue;

          slot.textContent = text;
          slot.hidden = false;
          el.hidden = true;
          return;
        }
      }

      function buildToc() {
        var body = document.querySelector('.shopify-policy__body');
        var toc = root.querySelector('[data-sv-toc]');
        var list = root.querySelector('[data-sv-toclist]');
        var heads = body ? Array.prototype.slice.call(body.querySelectorAll('h3')) : [];

        /* Under three sections a contents list is furniture, not
           navigation. */
        if (!toc || !list || heads.length < 3) return;

        var used = {};

        var slug = function (text) {
          return text.toLowerCase()
            .replace(/[‘’“”']/g, '')
            .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
            .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, '') || 'section';
        };

        /* "7. Opening your parcel" is the heading; "Opening your
           parcel" is what belongs in a narrow column. The number is
           still in the heading itself, doing its job. */
        var label = function (text) {
          return text.replace(/^\s*\d+[.)]\s*/, '').trim();
        };

        heads.forEach(function (h, i) {
          var text = (h.textContent || '').trim();
          if (!text) return;

          /* Prefixed so an id can never begin with a digit, and
             de-duplicated so two identically worded sections cannot
             both claim the same anchor. An id set by hand wins. */
          var id = h.id;
          if (!id) {
            id = 'p-' + slug(text);
            if (used[id]) id = id + '-' + (i + 1);
            h.id = id;
          }
          used[id] = true;

          var li = document.createElement('li');
          var a = document.createElement('a');
          a.className = 'sv-pol__toclink';
          a.href = '#' + id;
          a.textContent = label(text);
          li.appendChild(a);
          list.appendChild(li);
          marks.push({ head: h, link: a });
        });

        toc.hidden = false;
        spy();
      }

      /* ── Which section am I in ──────────────────────────────────────
         The contents list is only navigation while it is on screen, and
         on desktop it now is: the column is sticky beside a document
         that runs to twelve sections. A list of twelve identical links
         beside a page you are halfway down tells you nothing about
         where you are, which is the other half of what a contents list
         is for.

         Read off scroll rather than observed, deliberately. What is
         wanted is "the last heading that has passed under the header",
         which is one number; an IntersectionObserver reports entries
         and leaves you to reconstruct that number from them, and gets
         it wrong for any section shorter than the viewport — of which
         this document has several.

         Purely decorative: aria-current is set for a screen reader that
         is on the link, and nothing here moves focus or the page. */
      var marks = [];
      var current = null;
      var ticking = false;

      function spy() {
        ticking = false;
        if (!marks.length) return;

        var line = (parseInt(
          getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--sv-stick'), 10
        ) || 124) + 12;

        /* Before the first heading nothing is current — the lede is
           nobody's section — and the last one lights when its own
           heading passes the line like every other, not when the page
           bottoms out. Measuring the bottom would mean knowing which
           element scrolls, which is exactly the assumption this file
           has just been burned by. */
        var found = null;
        for (var i = 0; i < marks.length; i++) {
          if (marks[i].head.getBoundingClientRect().top <= line) found = marks[i];
          else break;
        }

        if (found === current) return;

        if (current) {
          current.link.classList.remove('is-here');
          current.link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
        }
        current = found;
        if (current) {
          current.link.classList.add('is-here');
          current.link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true');
          keepInView(current.link);
        }
      }

      /* On a long policy the live link can be outside the fold of the
         column's OWN scroll. Moved by hand rather than by
         scrollIntoView, which is free to scroll every scrollable
         ancestor — the window included — and would turn a reader's
         scroll into the page scrolling itself. This can only ever move
         the column, and only when the column actually scrolls. */
      function keepInView(link) {
        if (root.scrollHeight <= root.clientHeight + 4) return;

        var l = link.getBoundingClientRect();
        var box = root.getBoundingClientRect();

        if (l.top < box.top + 8) root.scrollTop += l.top - box.top - 8;
        else if (l.bottom > box.bottom - 8) root.scrollTop += l.bottom - box.bottom + 8;
      }

      /* ── Listening where the scrolling actually happens ─────────────
         This theme does not scroll the window. The page lives inside
         .page-wrapper, which is the element with overflow, so a
         window 'scroll' listener here never fires once — and the
         scroll-position arithmetic a spy would normally do against
         window.scrollY reads 0 for the whole length of the document.

         Registered on `document` in the CAPTURE phase instead. A scroll
         event does not bubble, but it does pass through the capture
         phase on its way down, so this one listener catches the window,
         .page-wrapper, and this column's own scroll alike — without
         this file naming, or having to keep up with, whichever of them
         the theme happens to use. */
      function queue() {
        if (ticking) return;
        ticking = true;
        requestAnimationFrame(spy);
      }

      document.addEventListener('scroll', queue, { capture: true, passive: true });
      window.addEventListener('resize', queue);

      /* ── A column may only be pinned beside something taller ────────
         Pinning is a bargain: the column gives up its place in the flow
         and, past the height of a screen, scrolls inside itself. That is
         worth it beside Privacy, which runs to twelve sections and five
         screens. Beside Contact it is a straight loss — the document is
         four short sections and the column is longer than it, so pinning
         puts two thirds of the column behind a scrollbar on a page that
         barely scrolls at all.

         Which it is cannot be known in CSS: it is the column's own
         height against the document's, and both are set by Admin copy.
         So it is measured, and the column is let back into the flow when
         it is the taller of the two. Re-measured on resize, because the
         document's height is a function of the width it is given.

         The class is dropped before measuring: with it on there is no
         clip, and scrollHeight would come back as the height of a box
         that is no longer holding anything back. */
      function fitColumn() {
        var doc = document.querySelector('.shopify-policy__container');
        if (!doc) return;

        root.classList.remove('sv-pol-mount--flow');
        if (root.scrollHeight > doc.offsetHeight - 40) {
          root.classList.add('sv-pol-mount--flow');
        }
      }

      /* All of these read the policy, and the policy is rendered after
         this column — see buildToc's note. Nothing here may run early.
         fitColumn goes first: the reveals it arms afterwards have to
         know whether the column is a scrollport before they choose what
         to measure "on screen" against. */
      function dressPolicy() {
        var body = document.querySelector('.shopify-policy__body');
        if (!body) return;
        dropRestatedTitle(body);
        liftStamp(body);
        buildToc();
        fitColumn();
        armReveals();
      }

      window.addEventListener('resize', fitColumn);

      if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', dressPolicy);
      } else {
        dressPolicy();
      }

      /* ── Reveal on scroll ───────────────────────────────────────────
         Same contract as sv-faq and sv-size-guide: fires once, then
         unobserves, and every held element is pinned to its finished
         state by a timer shortly after it should have arrived. A
         transition that never advances — a backgrounded tab, a dropped
         frame budget — must never be able to leave a policy's figures
         invisible. */
      function armReveals() {
        if (calm || !('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;

        var held = Array.prototype.slice.call(root.querySelectorAll('[data-sv-motion]'));

        var reveal = function (el) {
          if (el.classList.contains('sv-vis')) return;
          el.classList.add('sv-vis');
          mo.unobserve(el);
          setTimeout(function () { el.classList.add('sv-settled'); }, 1800);
        };

        /* ── Which fold ───────────────────────────────────────────────
           Above 900px this column is a scrollport of its own: held under
           the header, taller than the viewport, scrolling inside itself.
           Everything here lives inside it, so "on screen" means on
           screen IN THE COLUMN, not in the viewport — and an observer
           left on the viewport reports the journey and the hand-off as
           never intersecting, because they are clipped by a box that is
           itself fully in view. They would then be held at opacity 0
           permanently, in a column the reader can scroll to.

           Handing the observer that box as its root is the whole fix:
           the entrance then plays when the reader scrolls the column,
           which is the moment they arrive. Below 900px the wrapper is
           display:contents, has no box at all, and the root goes back
           to the viewport. */
        var clipped = function () {
          var oy = window.getComputedStyle(root).overflowY;
          return oy === 'auto' || oy === 'scroll';
        };

        var mo = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
          entries.forEach(function (en) { if (en.isIntersecting) reveal(en.target); });
        }, {
          root: clipped() ? root : null,
          rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px',
          threshold: 0.06
        });

        held.forEach(function (el) { mo.observe(el); });

        /* The eyebrow and the pins are above the fold by definition. */
        root.querySelectorAll('.sv-pol__eyebrow, .sv-pol__pins').forEach(reveal);

        /* An observer reports a state, not every state passed through:
           scroll hard enough in one gesture and an element can go from
           below the fold to above it without a callback ever firing,
           and would then stay invisible for good. */
        var queued = false;
        var sweep = function () {
          queued = false;
          /* Measured every time rather than cached: a window dragged
             across 900px turns the column from a scrollport into part
             of the page, and a fold remembered from load would go on
             holding whatever was below the old one. */
          var fold = clipped()
            ? root.getBoundingClientRect().bottom
            : window.innerHeight;

          held.forEach(function (el) {
            if (!el.classList.contains('sv-vis') &&
                el.getBoundingClientRect().top < fold) reveal(el);
          });
        };

        /* On `document`, in capture, for the reason the spy's listener
           above is: the window does not scroll on this theme, so the
           window-bound version of this backstop never ran and the 1.2s
           timer below was quietly carrying it alone. */
        document.addEventListener('scroll', function () {
          if (queued) return;
          queued = true;
          setTimeout(sweep, 180);
        }, { capture: true, passive: true });

        setTimeout(sweep, 1200);
      }
    })();
</script>

{% stylesheet %}
  /* Colour, type and spacing are tokens only. Primitives borrowed from
     sv-system.css: .eyebrow, .lab, .btn, .act. Nothing here redefines the
     system.

     Two scopes live in this file:
       · the PAGE layout, which has to reach Shopify's own container and
         so is written as `main:has(.sv-pol) …`. That compound (0,2,1)
         outranks sv-system.css's `.shopify-policy__container` (0,1,0)
         whichever of the two stylesheets the theme loads first, which is
         not a thing this section can otherwise know.
       · this column's own furniture, under .sv-pol.

     sv-system.css §02 scopes its reset under [data-sv] (0,1,1), which
     outranks a bare class (0,1,0) — every rule below that has to beat it
     is written compound for that reason, not for emphasis. */

  /* ── The page, once the column is present ───────────────────────────
     Shopify hands us <main> with the policy container inside it, and
     this section is rendered as that container's sibling. Nothing can be
     placed between the container's title and its body — the markup is
     Shopify's — so the summary reads as a column beside the document
     rather than as a band beneath its heading.

     The section's own wrapper is what the left column IS on desktop: one
     grid item holding both of its blocks, which is what lets it be
     sticky. Below 900px it is dissolved with display:contents so that
     its two blocks and the policy container become three siblings in one
     column and the document can sit between them — see the note on that
     breakpoint. Safe on this element and only this one: .shopify-section
     is a structural div Shopify adds, it carries no semantics, and it
     has no styles of its own to lose. */
  main:has(.sv-pol) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 270px minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--d);
    align-content: start;
    max-width: 1220px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--d) var(--c) var(--f);
    background: var(--cream);
  }

  /* ── The column travels ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     A contents list that scrolls away is usable once, at the top, and
     these documents run to twelve sections. Held under the header, it is
     usable at section nine — which is the only place anybody has ever
     wanted one.

     Sticky needs two things this used to break. The travel comes from
     the grid AREA, so the column has to be ONE item in a row as tall as
     the document — hence the wrapper doing the work rather than
     display:contents and two items in two rows. And it must not be
     stretched, hence align-self, or its box already fills the row and
     there is nowhere to travel to.

     Scrolling inside itself is the honest answer to a column that is
     occasionally taller than the viewport — Privacy carries figures, a
     clause and a journey — and it costs nothing on the pages where it
     never binds. dvh rather than vh so a phone's collapsing address bar
     does not leave the foot of it unreachable; 900px down it is not
     sticky at all, but the value is written once. */
  .sv-pol-mount {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1;
    align-self: start;
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--sv-stick) + var(--b));
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--b);
    max-height: calc(100dvh - var(--sv-stick) - var(--c));
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* overflow-y:auto makes overflow-x `auto` too, which clips. The
       padding is there so that a focus ring — 2px at a 4px offset — is
       not sheared off against the column's own edge, and the matching
       negative margin keeps the content on the 270px track. Scroll
       chaining is deliberately left at its default: contained, a wheel
       over the column would stop dead at the foot of it instead of
       carrying on down the page, which is what a reader is doing. */
    padding: 4px 8px;
    margin: -4px -8px;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: var(--cream-2) transparent;
  }

  /* Set by the script when the column turns out to be taller than the
     document beside it — Contact, which is four short sections. Pinning
     is only worth its cost beside something long enough to read past it;
     here the column simply runs down the page as it used to, and the
     page scrolls both. */
  .sv-pol-mount--flow {
    position: static;
    max-height: none;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  .sv-pol-mount::-webkit-scrollbar {
    width: 4px;
  }

  .sv-pol-mount::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
    background: var(--cream-2);
    border-radius: 999px;
  }

  .sv-pol__after {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--b);
    align-content: start;
    background: transparent;
  }

  main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__container {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
  }

  /* The container centres itself and carries its own page padding when
     it is the whole page. As a grid cell it must do neither. */
  main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__container {
    max-width: none;
    margin-inline: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Shopify's <h1> is the page title and stays exactly where it is; the
     eyebrow above it belongs to this column, so the two are aligned by
     killing the leading offset rather than by moving either. */
  main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__container .shopify-policy__title {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* Anchors have to land clear of the sticky header group. --sv-stick is
     measured onto <html> by the script; the value here is the fallback. */
  :root {
    --sv-stick: 124px;
  }

  main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__body h2,
  main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__body h3 {
    scroll-margin-top: var(--sv-stick);
  }

  /* ── The column ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  [data-sv].sv-pol {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--b);
    align-content: start;
    background: transparent;
  }

  [data-sv] .sv-pol__eyebrow {
    /* Optically level with the serif <h1> beside it, which sits on a
       1.06 line-height and so starts higher than its box does. */
    margin-top: 6px;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ── The stamp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Sits directly under the eyebrow and reads as its second line, which
     is what it is: what this document is for, and when it last changed.
     Small, tabular, ink — a date is a fact, not an accent, and the gold
     it would otherwise have taken fails contrast at this size (the
     argument the labels below make in full). The negative top margin
     closes the column's var(--b) gap: these two lines are one unit. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__stamp {
    margin-top: calc(var(--a) - var(--b));
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--ink);
    opacity: 0.72;
  }

  /* ── Labels, made readable ──────────────────────────────────────────
     .lab paints gold (#C6A15B) at .66rem. On the ivory card that is
     2.4:1 and on the cream ground 2.5:1 — against AA's 4.5:1 for text
     this size. .lab--grey and .eyebrow are no better at 4.2:1. The
     system already states the principle at §05, "Gold is never a button
     or a link — contrast fails"; it just was not carried through to
     small caps on a light ground.

     So on this page gold is kept for everything that is a rule or an
     edge, where contrast is not a legibility question — the pins' cap
     bars, the numeral rings, the title's rule, the list markers — and
     every label takes the reading pass's own answer instead
     (sv-system.css §03c, .sv-read-lab): ink at 600, which is 17:1.
     Hierarchy then comes from size, weight and case rather than colour.

     Compound on purpose: .lab is (0,1,0) and this must beat it whichever
     of the two stylesheets the theme happens to load first. */
  .sv-pol .lab,
  .sv-pol__after .lab {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ── Contents ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .sv-pol__toclab {
    margin-bottom: var(--a);
  }

  [data-sv] .sv-pol__toclist {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--cream-2);
  }

  .sv-pol__toclink {
    display: block;
    padding: 7px 0 7px var(--a);
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    /* These are the page's navigation, so they take ink (16:1) rather
       than --grey, which is 4.2:1 on the cream ground. */
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--std) var(--e);
  }

  .sv-pol__toclink:hover,
  .sv-pol__toclink:focus-visible {
    color: var(--emerald);
  }

  /* ── Where you are ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Gold, on the list's own left rule — a mark on an edge, not text, so
     it is the one place on this page gold is allowed to carry meaning
     (the column's contrast argument, made once below, holds: gold is
     kept for rules and edges and never for type). The label alongside
     turns emerald and gains weight, which is what actually does the
     work; the bar is what makes it findable without reading. */
  .sv-pol__toclink.is-here {
    color: var(--emerald);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .sv-pol__toclist li {
    position: relative;
  }

  .sv-pol__toclink.is-here::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -1px;
    top: 4px;
    bottom: 4px;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--tan);
  }

  /* [hidden] is only a UA rule at display:none, so any class here that
     sets a display silently beats it. One guard for the column rather
     than remembering it at each new display declaration. */
  .sv-pol [hidden] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* ── Pins ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__pins {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--a);
  }

  .sv-pol__pin {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: var(--b) var(--a);
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: var(--r-img);
    box-shadow: var(--sh-s);
  }

  /* The gold cap is a bar, not a border, so it can draw. */
  .sv-pol__pin::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--tan);
    border-radius: var(--r-img) var(--r-img) 0 0;
  }

  .sv-pol__pinval {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--emerald);
  }

  /* --grey on the card is 4.4:1 — a hair under AA at this size, and this
     is the line that qualifies the figure above it, so it has to be
     read, not skimmed past. Ink, one step smaller than the body. */
  .sv-pol__pinnote {
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ── The video rule · refund only ───────────────────────────────────
     The page's one dark object. Everything else on a policy page is ink
     or emerald on ivory, so emerald-on-emerald is the only weight left
     that does not mean "error" — and this is not an error, it is the
     condition the whole document rests on.

     It takes the pins' gold cap rather than the help card's left rule,
     because it belongs to the figures above it: those three say what you
     get, this says what you must have done to get it. */
  .sv-pol__rule {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    justify-items: start;
    padding: var(--b) var(--a);
    background: var(--emerald);
    border-radius: var(--r-img);
    box-shadow: var(--sh-s);
  }

  .sv-pol__rule::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--tan);
    border-radius: var(--r-img) var(--r-img) 0 0;
  }

  /* The system's default outline is gold, which all but vanishes on this
     ground — the same swap .panel-dark makes in sv-system.css §10. */
  .sv-pol__rule :focus-visible {
    outline-color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* Gold small caps on emerald is 3.2:1 and this column has already made
     that argument once, above: gold stays on the cap bar, where contrast
     is not a legibility question, and the label takes cream at 8.4:1.
     Label and heading then share a colour, and the hierarchy is carried
     by size, case and face instead — which is how the rest of the column
     already does it. Softened one step so the two do not read as one
     run-on line; still 5.8:1. */
  .sv-pol .sv-pol__rule .lab {
    color: rgba(251, 247, 239, 0.78);
  }

  /* Both cards are a <p> label directly above a <p> heading, and
     `[data-sv] p + p` (0,1,1) is a PAGE rhythm — var(--b), 28px. Landing
     between a label and the thing it labels it reads as two unrelated
     lines rather than one; measured at 38px against the 10px the grid
     gap asks for. Zeroed so the gap is the only thing spacing these.
     Direct children only: the richtext bodies below set their own. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__rule > p + p,
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__help > p + p {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* Compound for the same reason .sv-pol__helph is: this is a <p>, and
     `[data-sv] p` (0,1,1) sets --grey and would win against a bare
     class — on this ground that is 2.1:1 and unreadable. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__ruleh {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* 7.2:1. The measure is a cap rather than the system's `none`: on the
     270px column it never binds, but between 640px and 900px this card
     is the full width of the page and the line ran to 95 characters. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__rulebody p {
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: rgba(251, 247, 239, 0.9);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  /* --b between two paragraphs is a page rhythm, not a card's. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__rulebody p + p {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  [data-sv] .sv-pol__rulebody em {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.06em;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* .act would paint ink here. Same shape — caps, tracked, gold rule
     under it — rebuilt in the one colour that reads on this ground. */
  .sv-pol__rulelink {
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--cream);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--tan);
    padding-bottom: 6px;
    transition: opacity var(--micro) var(--e);
  }

  .sv-pol__rulelink:hover,
  .sv-pol__rulelink:focus-visible {
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  /* ── The journey ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* .lab alone loses this one: sv-system.css §03 sets the serif face and
     a clamp()ed display size on `[data-sv] h2` (0,1,1), which outranks a
     bare class, so the label rendered as a 3rem serif heading. It stays
     an <h2> because the strip is a titled section; the class is restated
     compound so it wins. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__flowh {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  [data-sv] .sv-pol__steps {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--b);
    margin-top: var(--a);
  }

  /* The thread the numerals hang from. Stopped short at both ends so it
     begins and finishes at a numeral rather than running past them. */
  .sv-pol__thread {
    position: absolute;
    left: 12px;
    top: 12px;
    bottom: 12px;
    width: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--tan), rgba(198, 161, 91, 0.3));
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .sv-pol__step {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
    padding-left: calc(26px + var(--a));
    min-height: 26px;
  }

  /* The numeral caps the thread. Its ivory ground is what makes the line
     look interrupted rather than crossed out. */
  .sv-pol__stepnum {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 26px;
    height: 26px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--cream);
    border: 1px solid var(--tan);
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--emerald);
  }

  .sv-pol__steplab {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .sv-pol__stepdet {
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* ── Where to go next ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .sv-pol__help {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
    justify-items: start;
    padding: var(--b) var(--a);
    background: var(--card);
    border-radius: var(--r-img);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--tan);
    box-shadow: var(--sh-s);
  }

  /* Compound: this is a <p>, and `[data-sv] p` (0,1,1) sets --grey and
     outranks a bare class, so the emerald never landed and the heading
     was sitting at 4.4:1 while looking intentional. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__helph {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--emerald);
  }

  /* Capped rather than `none` for the reason the rule card's body is:
     the column is 270px on desktop, where this never binds, and the full
     width of the page between 640px and 900px, where it did. */
  [data-sv] .sv-pol__helpbody p {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .sv-pol__helpbtn {
    /* A 46px-padded button does not fit a 270px column. */
    padding: 14px var(--b);
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 6px;
  }

  /* The second action, where a card has two. The 6px above is the gap
     between the body and the buttons; repeated between the buttons it
     separates a pair that should read as one. */
  .sv-pol__helpmail {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  /* One button sizes to its own words, which is right when it is alone.
     Two do not: "Message on WhatsApp" is half again as long as "Send an
     email", and left to themselves they stack as two different widths
     with a ragged right edge inside a card whose every other edge is
     flush. Stretched only where a second button exists. */
  .sv-pol__help:has(.sv-pol__helpmail) .sv-pol__helpbtn {
    justify-self: stretch;
  }

  .sv-pol__helplink {
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  /* ── Entrances ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Each element arrives on the system's --stagger; the pins' caps draw
     in behind them. Three cards landing together read as one block,
     which is exactly what they are not. */
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready [data-sv-motion],
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins > li {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(var(--rise));
    transition: opacity 0.7s var(--entrance), transform 0.7s var(--entrance);
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready [data-sv-motion].sv-vis,
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li:nth-child(2) {
    transition-delay: var(--stagger);
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li:nth-child(3) {
    transition-delay: calc(var(--stagger) * 2);
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins > li::before,
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__rule::before {
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: left center;
    transition: transform 0.75s var(--e);
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li::before,
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__rule.sv-vis::before {
    transform: none;
    transition-delay: 0.25s;
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li:nth-child(2)::before {
    transition-delay: calc(0.25s + var(--stagger));
  }

  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-pol__pins.sv-vis > li:nth-child(3)::before {
    transition-delay: calc(0.25s + var(--stagger) * 2);
  }

  /* A held element pinned by the script's timer is visible whatever the
     transition did. */
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-settled,
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-settled > li {
    opacity: 1 !important;
    transform: none !important;
  }

  /* The caps draw with their own transform, so the guard above — which
     only reaches the element — cannot land them. Left out, a dropped
     frame budget leaves the refund page's one gold mark at scaleX(0). */
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-settled::before,
  .sv-pol-mount.is-js.sv-motion-ready .sv-settled > li::before {
    transform: none !important;
  }

  /* ── Responsive ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    main:has(.sv-pol) {
      grid-template-columns: 240px minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--c);
    }
  }

  /* One column, and the reason the section renders as two blocks: the
     page opens with the eyebrow, the contents and the figures, then the
     document with its own title, then the journey and the hand-off.
     Everything in one block would have put that title three screens
     down a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    main:has(.sv-pol) {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--c);
      padding: var(--c) var(--b) var(--d);
    }

    /* The wrapper is dissolved and its two blocks become grid items of
       <main> alongside the policy container, which is the only way the
       document can sit BETWEEN them — and it has to, or a phone opens
       the page on three screens of summary before the policy's own
       title. Sticky goes with it: a column held under the header while
       the document scrolls past is a two-column idea, and in one column
       it would simply cover the page. */
    .sv-pol-mount {
      display: contents;
      position: static;
      max-height: none;
      overflow: visible;
    }

    .sv-pol,
    .sv-pol__after,
    main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__container {
      grid-column: auto;
      grid-row: auto;
    }

    .sv-pol { order: 1; }
    main:has(.sv-pol) .shopify-policy__container { order: 2; }
    .sv-pol__after { order: 3; }

    [data-sv].sv-pol {
      gap: var(--b);
    }

    /* Ten stacked links is a screen of its own on a phone. Wrapped, the
       same list is three rows. */
    [data-sv] .sv-pol__toclist {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 6px 8px;
      border-left: 0;
    }

    .sv-pol__toclink {
      padding: 6px 12px;
      background: var(--card);
      border-radius: 999px;
      box-shadow: var(--sh-s);
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    [data-sv] .sv-pol__pins {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }

    .sv-pol__helpbtn {
      justify-self: start;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    [data-sv] .sv-pol__pins {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

